• ## Root cause

  `run-uncovered?` correctly evaluates to `false` at `src/dev/node_shard.clj:45-49`, but **it is never referenced**.

  At `src/dev/node_shard.clj:147`, shard creation is gated only by whether uncovered namespaces exist:

  ```clojure
  lists (cond-> lists (seq uncovered)
                (assoc "uncovered" (vec uncovered)))
  ```

  Because the light subsystem list leaves 176 namespaces uncovered, `(seq uncovered)` is true. This writes `ns-uncovered.txt`; the subsequent loader adds that file to `lists`, and the scheduler executes it. The `cond->` syntax, lexical scopes, and environment-variable name are all correct—the predicate simply omits `run-uncovered?`.

  ## Minimal fix

  One-line change at `src/dev/node_shard.clj:147`:

  ```clojure
  lists (cond-> lists (and run-uncovered? (seq uncovered))
                (assoc "uncovered" (vec uncovered)))
  ```

  No later gate is required because `ns-uncovered.txt` will no longer be created when skipped.

  ## Other lanes

  - **Dart has the same bug.** `src/dev/dart_shard.clj:118-119` adds the residual solely on `(seq uncovered)`, with no skip flag. Add the same `run-uncovered?` definition used by Node and gate this expression with `(and run-uncovered? (seq uncovered))`.
  - **JVM does not have this pattern.** `src/dev/nightly.clj` has no uncovered-residual shard. Its subset handling runs quick tests only for assigned subsystems (`:164-170`) and filters slow namespaces by that subset (`:159-160`).

  Read-only diagnosis only; no files were changed or committed.

